<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Store Network cockpit: 330 stores plotted by real coordinates, marker size
     = sales per square foot, colour split for stores with 2+ competitors
     within 2 km.

     TECHNIQUE, decided empirically per the task brief's 3-tier discovery
     budget (2 redeploy cycles max before settling, matching the
     waterfall/heatmap precedent):

     TIER 1 TRIED AND REJECTED - community jr:map (Map component,
     xmlns:jr="http://jasperreports.sourceforge.net/jasperreports/components").
     Two things converge to rule it out:
       1. LOCAL: jasperreports-lib (the machine's JR_LIB_DIR) has only
          jasperreports-7.0.6, jasperreports-charts-7.0.6, jfreechart-1.5.6,
          jasperreports-barcode4j-7.0.6, jasperreports-pdf-7.0.6 and their
          transitive deps (confirmed by directory listing) - there is no
          jasperreports-components jar, which is the module that registers the
          Map/List/Table component namespace's XML digester rules and the
          MapComponent class itself. A jr:map jrxml therefore fails to even
          load locally: "Unable to load report" with no further Jackson detail
          (JRXmlLoader.loadXML throws before reaching a property-level error,
          because the namespace has no registered rule set at all, not because
          a property name is wrong). This directly contradicts the task
          brief's assumption that this component is "locally compilable" -
          that assumption held for the OTHER community components already in
          this suite (chart, crosstab, spiderChart, barcode4j), which DO have
          their jars here, but not for jr:map specifically.
       2. SERVER: two discrete redeploy attempts (the full budget) both got
          the exact opaque 400 "JRXML.content is invalid" that
          fusion-pro-gotchas.md documents as the signature of an
          unavailable/misregistered component, not a fixable property typo -
          attempt 1 used a bare jr:markerDataset with direct
          markerLatitudeExpression/markerLongitudeExpression children; attempt
          2 wrapped those in a jr:markerSeries (the more common form seen in
          Jaspersoft Studio-generated samples). Neither changed the error text
          at all, which is the tell: a real property-name mismatch on a
          present component surfaces a specific Jackson complaint (as seen
          throughout this suite for JR7-native elements), while a genuinely
          absent/unregistered component namespace surfaces this same generic
          400 regardless of what is inside it. Combined with finding 1 (the
          component plainly is not part of this environment's assembled
          jar set, matching reports.md's Community/Pro tables, neither of
          which lists Map/List/Table anywhere in this repo), the conclusion is
          that jr:map is not available on this STAGE server either, not that
          the schema was wrong. Budget spent: exactly 2 redeploy cycles, as
          allowed.

     TIER 2 SHIPPED - JFreeChart chartType="bubble" (dataset kind="xyz"),
     JR7-native element kind="chart" terse form, no extra jar beyond the
     jfreechart-1.5.6.jar already proven in this suite (pnl_waterfall.jrxml,
     lab_format.jrxml). Compiles locally, entirely offline, once two local
     issues were fixed (see COMMENT GOTCHA and FIELD-NAME GOTCHA below) - no
     server round trip needed at all to validate the XML shape, which is
     exactly the payoff the brief called out for a true community component,
     in sharp contrast to tier 1's two burned server round trips for a
     component this environment does not have. Longitude on x, latitude on y,
     sales_per_sqft on z (bubble radius) - a closer literal match to the
     brief's "bubble size from sales per square foot" than a flat xyScatter
     dataset (kind="xy") could give, since kind="xy" carries no third value to
     size a marker by at all; the brief's prose asked for bubble sizing
     specifically, so "bubble" (kind="xyz") is the type that actually
     delivers it, and it sits in the same xy-chart family the brief pointed
     at. The colour split for the "ring" condition (competitors_2km >= 2) is
     two series over the SAME query, each returning null x/y/z for rows
     outside its own condition (the JR-native way to partition one row source
     into two rendered series, same technique pnl_waterfall.jrxml uses for
     its bridge bars) - this is an XY dataset, not a category dataset, so the
     seriesColor first-registration hazard documented in pnl_waterfall.jrxml
     does NOT apply here (that hazard is specific to category datasets where
     a series occupies a row-indexed slot; an xy/xyz dataset's series are
     static design-time declarations that each independently walk every row,
     so a null point on one series's first few rows does not shift any other
     series's colour binding). Loses the basemap (no province/street imagery
     under the points), which is the known and accepted trade-off of this
     tier per the brief.

     COMMENT GOTCHA hit while building this exact file: the first local
     compile of the tier-2 draft failed with the same bare "Unable to load
     report" as tier 1, no cause attached (JR7's JacksonReportLoader logs the
     real cause via SLF4J and JRXmlLoader re-throws without it; this
     environment's slf4j-api has no bound provider, so the log line is
     silently dropped - recovered it only by adding slf4j-simple-1.8.0-beta4
     to the classpath for one throwaway debug run). The real cause:
     com.ctc.wstx.exc.WstxParsingException, reporting that a literal double
     hyphen is not allowed inside an XML comment - an earlier draft of THIS
     header comment used exactly that as a prose dash, the same suite-wide
     gotcha called out in the task brief. Left in as a live example of why
     that check matters even for a file whose component tier turned out to
     need none of the doubted schema work.

     FIELD-NAME GOTCHA, found immediately after fixing the comment: the first
     real compile attempt then gave a genuine, precise Jackson complaint
     exactly as fusion-pro-gotchas.md predicts for a present community
     component - UnrecognizedPropertyException on JRDesignXyzSeries naming
     its five real properties: xvalueExpression, yvalueExpression,
     zvalueExpression, seriesExpression, itemHyperlink. The lower-case v
     matters - xValueExpression (capital V, the form used on every category
     dataset series in this suite, e.g. pnl_waterfall's valueExpression) is
     REJECTED on an xyz series; JRDesignXyzSeries's Jackson bean names come
     from its getX/Y/Zvalue() getters, not getX/Y/ZValue(). Corrected and
     recompiled clean. (Confirmed later, while building net_income_scatter.jrxml,
     that the plain xy series class - JRDesignXySeries - has the exact same
     lower-case-v naming; it is not an xyz-only quirk.)

     RENDER-SCALE GOTCHA, found on the first run-to-PDF: the raw
     sales_per_sqft value (roughly 700 to 1300) fed straight into
     zvalueExpression rendered as a SOLID BLUE RECTANGLE filling the entire
     plot, not visible bubbles - JFreeChart's bubble renderer here treats z as
     bubble AREA in the same data-space units as the x/y axes (radius =
     sqrt(z / pi)), not auto-scaled pixels, so a store at z=1000 draws a
     circle of radius about 17.8 DEGREES on a plot whose full longitude span
     is about 90 degrees; with 330 overlapping circles that size the union
     covers the whole rectangle. Divided sales_per_sqft by 1000 in both
     series' zvalueExpression (BigDecimal.divide, scale 4, HALF_UP) to bring
     z into the roughly 0.7-1.3 range, giving bubble radii of roughly
     0.5-0.6 degrees - small, distinct dots at this plot's scale. This is a
     display-only transform; the underlying values are unaffected everywhere
     else in the suite (net_sqft_format.jrxml shows the real, unscaled
     numbers).

     Standard stores: #0550DC (established series-blue). Ringed stores (2+
     competitors within 2 km, 26 of 330 - verified 2026-08-25): #F0AD4E (the
     existing amber/warning accent already used for gauge zones in
     exec_margin_dial.jrxml / exec_yoy_dial.jrxml), so the break-out reads as
     an alert rather than just a second neutral category. Chart lives in the
     title band, hence evaluationTime="Report". Cockpit archetype: no
     parameters. -->
<jasperReport name="net_map" pageWidth="880" pageHeight="680" columnWidth="860" leftMargin="10" rightMargin="10" topMargin="10" bottomMargin="10">

	<query language="SQL"><![CDATA[
SELECT s.storenumber, s.storename, s.latitude, s.longitude, s.region,
       DECIMAL(FLOAT8(s.total_sales) / FLOAT8(NULLIF(s.square_feet, 0)), 10, 2) AS sales_per_sqft,
       (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM competitor_locations c WHERE c.storenumber = s.storenumber AND c.distance_km <= 2) AS competitors_2km
FROM stores s
	]]></query>

	<field name="storenumber" class="java.lang.Integer"/>
	<field name="storename" class="java.lang.String"/>
	<field name="latitude" class="java.math.BigDecimal"/>
	<field name="longitude" class="java.math.BigDecimal"/>
	<field name="region" class="java.lang.String"/>
	<field name="sales_per_sqft" class="java.math.BigDecimal"/>
	<field name="competitors_2km" class="java.lang.Long"/>

	<title height="660">
		<element kind="image" x="0" y="0" width="44" height="44" scaleImage="RetainShape" hImageAlign="Left" vImageAlign="Top"><expression><![CDATA["repo:/images/actian_logo"]]></expression></element>
		<element kind="staticText" x="0" y="0" width="860" height="24" fontSize="16.0" bold="true" forecolor="#1F4E79" hTextAlign="Center"><text><![CDATA[Store Network]]></text></element>
		<element kind="staticText" x="0" y="26" width="860" height="14" fontSize="9.0" forecolor="#5B7DA6" hTextAlign="Center"><text><![CDATA[Marker size = sales per square foot; ringed markers have 2 or more competitors within 2 km]]></text></element>

		<element kind="chart" chartType="bubble" x="0" y="48" width="860" height="600" showLegend="true" evaluationTime="Report">
			<dataset kind="xyz">
				<series>
					<seriesExpression><![CDATA["Standard"]]></seriesExpression>
					<xvalueExpression><![CDATA[($F{competitors_2km} != null && $F{competitors_2km}.longValue() >= 2) ? null : $F{longitude}]]></xvalueExpression>
					<yvalueExpression><![CDATA[($F{competitors_2km} != null && $F{competitors_2km}.longValue() >= 2) ? null : $F{latitude}]]></yvalueExpression>
					<zvalueExpression><![CDATA[($F{competitors_2km} != null && $F{competitors_2km}.longValue() >= 2) ? null : ($F{sales_per_sqft} == null ? java.math.BigDecimal.ZERO : $F{sales_per_sqft}.divide(new java.math.BigDecimal(1000), 4, java.math.RoundingMode.HALF_UP))]]></zvalueExpression>
				</series>
				<series>
					<seriesExpression><![CDATA["2+ Competitors within 2 km"]]></seriesExpression>
					<xvalueExpression><![CDATA[($F{competitors_2km} != null && $F{competitors_2km}.longValue() >= 2) ? $F{longitude} : null]]></xvalueExpression>
					<yvalueExpression><![CDATA[($F{competitors_2km} != null && $F{competitors_2km}.longValue() >= 2) ? $F{latitude} : null]]></yvalueExpression>
					<zvalueExpression><![CDATA[($F{competitors_2km} != null && $F{competitors_2km}.longValue() >= 2) ? ($F{sales_per_sqft} == null ? java.math.BigDecimal.ZERO : $F{sales_per_sqft}.divide(new java.math.BigDecimal(1000), 4, java.math.RoundingMode.HALF_UP)) : null]]></zvalueExpression>
				</series>
			</dataset>
			<plot>
				<seriesColor order="0" color="#0550DC"/>
				<seriesColor order="1" color="#F0AD4E"/>
			</plot>
		</element>
	</title>

</jasperReport>
